---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import RecentWork from "../../components/RecentWork.astro";

import jetpackPartner from "../../assets/jetpack-partner.png";
import pressablePartner from "../../assets/pressable-partner.png";
import wooPartner from "../../assets/woo-partner.png";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Custom WordPress Design &amp; Development | Kelp"
  description="Custom WordPress websites, content systems, integrations, migrations, and ongoing support designed around your team&#x27;s workflow."
  darkHeader={true}
>
  <article>
    <SimpleHero
      title="WordPress"
      description="Kelp is a proud Automattic Agency partner. This means we are experts within the WordPress ecosystem including Jetpack, Pressable, and WooCommerce. In fact, this very website is a headless WordPress instance."
      dark={true}
    />

    <section class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <div class="platform">
        <div class="platform-logo">
          <Image
            src={jetpackPartner}
            alt="Jetpack Pro Partner"
            height={133}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <Image
            src={pressablePartner}
            alt="Pressable Pro Partner"
            height={133}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <Image
            src={wooPartner}
            alt="WooCommerce Pro Partner"
            height={133}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="platform-text">
          <p>
            We love building custom WordPress themes and plugins with a clear
            purpose. WordPress can be an incredibly powerful
            platform&thinsp;—&thinsp;as long as you respect its ecosystem and
            use it the way it was designed. We’ve embraced the Gutenberg editor
            and gone all-in on block-based themes.
            <a href="https://woocommerce.com/posts/the-wooexpert-awards-americas-2023/">
              Automattic recognized our work
            </a>{" "}
            with <strong>The Innovator Award in 2023</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We believe that your website's underlying technology can either hold
            you back, or give you momentum to hit new records. Our preferred
            method to build custom WordPress themes is with{" "}
            <a href="https://upstatement.com/timber">Timber</a> as it provides a
            powerful templating engine with a clean syntax. However, we don't
            mind working in existing themes built with good ol' vanilla PHP.
            Headless WordPress sounds scary, but it can also be quite nice.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="service-body">
      <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
        <h2>Where Teams Get Stuck</h2>
        <p>
          WordPress becomes difficult when editing is inconsistent, plugins
          accumulate without a plan, or custom code is undocumented. A site may
          look fine to visitors while the team avoids making needed updates.
        </p>
        <h2>How We Fix It</h2>
        <p>
          We organize the content and block system around what editors actually
          publish. Custom themes, plugins, integrations, or a headless front end
          are considered when they solve a defined problem. We also plan
          performance, accessibility, updates, and support from the outset.
        </p>
        <h2>Build for the Content You Really Manage</h2>
        <p>
          We organize pages, custom content, navigation, and reusable components
          so the site can grow without becoming difficult to update. The build
          can include custom interfaces, application connections, forms, and
          migrations where the workflow requires them.
        </p>
        <h2>Keep the Experience Consistent</h2>
        <p>
          In <a href="/work/artisan-talent/">Artisan Talent</a>, Kelp worked with a custom WordPress setup, an
          Angular jobs application, and HubSpot pages to maintain a unified
          experience. Ongoing updates and support are part of keeping a
          WordPress site reliable. Talk with Kelp about your WordPress project.
        </p>
        <p>
          Kelp's own website is a headless WordPress build. The team also works
          with block-based editing, custom themes, and plugins. The architecture
          depends on how editors work, what the site must connect to, and the
          long-term cost of maintaining it.
        </p>
        <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
        <p>
          WordPress is flexible, but that flexibility needs an editing model. We
          decide which content should be structured, which layouts should be
          reusable, and where custom development is justified. Performance,
          accessibility, search access, and maintenance are part of the build
          plan. Integrations with applications or marketing systems are scoped
          around ownership of data and the workflow people actually use.
        </p>
        <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Content architecture and editor experience</li>
          <li>Custom theme or block development</li>
          <li>Integrations and migration</li>
          <li>Maintenance and performance plan</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
        <p>
          Show us the current editing process and the changes the team cannot
          make confidently. We can decide whether targeted improvements or a
          broader rebuild will serve you better.
        </p>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <h3>Do we need headless WordPress?</h3>
        <p>
          Only when its flexibility justifies the added complexity. Kelp's own
          site uses headless WordPress, but a conventional build may be a better
          fit for many teams.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
            Talk Through Your Project
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <RecentWork
      tag="88"
      limit={13}
      heading="WordPress Sites"
      description="<p>Don't just take our word for it, check out some of our real projects in the carousel below.</p>"
    />
    <FeaturedArticles
      category="11"
      heading="Our Articles about WordPress"
      limit={3}
    />
  </article>
  <style lang="scss">
    .platform {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 2rem 6rem;
      margin: 1rem 0;
      padding: 4rem 0;
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--teal);
      &:last-child {
        border-bottom: 0;
      }
      &-logo {
        flex: 0 1 220px;
        text-align: center;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 2rem;
        order: 2;
        @media (max-width: 767px) {
          margin: auto;
        }
      }
      &-text {
        flex: 1 1 500px;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
